\DOC ASSUME_CALLEE_SAFETY_TAC

\TYPE {ASSUME_CALLEE_SAFETY_TAC : thm -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Adds the body of an existentially quantified safety theorem to the goal's
assumptions, with the existential witness replaced by a freshly named constant,
leaving the conclusion untouched.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes {callee_safety_proof : thm}, a theorem of the form
{|- ?f_events. t}, and {asmname : string}, the label to attach to the new
assumption (if the string is empty, the assumption is added unlabelled). It
strips the leading existential quantifier of the theorem and substitutes a
fresh distinct name for the quantified variable, yielding {u}, the instantiated
body of {th}; {u} is a universally quantified implication over the remaining
bound variables {x1 ... xn}. The conclusion is left exactly as it was, and
existing assumptions are kept, so repeated applications accumulate several such
assumptions.
{
           A ?- t
   ======================================  ASSUME_CALLEE_SAFETY_TAC (|- ?f_events. t') asmname
         A, u ?- t
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single kind of outcome occurs, with goals
whose conclusion is an existentially quantified statement of the same shape as
the theorem argument.

\ENDDOC
